WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:08.300
So now that we know what simulates the will of the AI agent, it's time to look at what drives the logic and the memory behind the AI agent.

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.599
The Behaviour Tree can be best described as the brain of the AI.

3
00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:16.899
In fact, the Behaviour Tree component inherits directly from the brain component.

4
00:00:16.899 --> 00:00:23.879
The Behaviour Tree component is held by the AI controller and indeed makes a clear assumption that it's owned by an AI controller,

5
00:00:23.879 --> 00:00:28.750
with functions such as GetAIOwner being made available to programmers.

6
00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:36.750
It's also strongly paired with a Blackboard component, exposing functions to developers that allow us to retrieve the Blackboard component from the Behaviour Tree directly.

7
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:42.390
It's important to note that although the ownership pairing has been established in the code architecture,

8
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.390
the AI controller does not come with a brain component already attached.

9
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:50.159
This can be done by a developer through Blueprint or C++, however.

10
00:00:50.159 --> 00:00:53.159
Lastly, if you choose to add the Behaviour Tree component through Blueprint,

11
00:00:53.159 --> 00:01:01.159
the native C++ code of the AI controller will automatically search for a brain component on itself and set its own member variables accordingly.

12
00:01:01.159 --> 00:01:08.700
The Behaviour Tree component allows developers to plug in their desired Behaviour Tree in the editor and have it be run immediately upon possession.

13
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:16.500
Alternatively, we can explicitly call RunBehaviourTree ourselves and pass through a specific tree as and when we find it suitable.

14
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.819
If a Behaviour Tree component is not present when calling RunBehaviourTree, then one will be created for you.

15
00:01:22.819 --> 00:01:28.650
The Behaviour Tree component is in charge of running our AI logic, that being the Behaviour Tree asset.

16
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:33.719
As already mentioned, it will start the AI logic on possession and stop AI logic on unpossess.

17
00:01:33.719 --> 00:01:36.719
It will also tick the Behaviour Tree directly for us.

18
00:01:36.719 --> 00:01:39.719
We can also use it to pause the Behaviour Tree's logic.

19
00:01:39.719 --> 00:01:46.930
The brain component on the AI also features a special messaging system that the AI logic can utilise.

20
00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.349
You'll find examples of these already in the engine.

21
00:01:50.349 --> 00:01:55.349
These messages allow us to know what may have gone on during the processing of a piece of AI logic.

22
00:01:55.349 --> 00:02:01.920
For example, in our MoveTo task, we can determine whether or not we succeeded or failed in our move.

23
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:08.500
This will determine whether or not we proceed onto the next node in the tree or fail the whole node in its entirety.

24
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:13.500
A Behaviour Tree allows an AI agent to do their thinking and make decisions that drive their functionality.

25
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.659
A Behaviour Tree can be paired with a Blackboard to efficiently make decisions, although it isn't strictly necessary to use one.

26
00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:22.659
However, it is still highly recommended that you do use a Blackboard.

27
00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:29.979
As we mentioned previously, the Blackboard component and the Behaviour Tree component have been strongly paired in the code architecture.

28
00:02:29.979 --> 00:02:32.979
Behaviour Trees are created within the editor, as can be seen in the image here.

29
00:02:32.979 --> 00:02:41.199
And we simply right-click in the Content Browser, go to the Artificial Intelligence category, and select the Behaviour Tree option.

30
00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:47.770
Behaviour Trees are built in a hierarchical set of nodes that control the flow of decision making, which we're going to cover shortly.

31
00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:52.770
In Unreal, Behaviour Trees are event-driven, which avoids doing unnecessary polling every frame.

32
00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:58.509
The Behaviour Tree passively listens for events that can then be used to trigger changes in the tree.

33
00:02:58.509 --> 00:03:03.509
This type of code architecture means that we do not need to iterate over the entire tree every tick,

34
00:03:03.509 --> 00:03:06.509
so instead we rely on the messages to inform us of these changes.

35
00:03:06.509 --> 00:03:14.370
They also help with debugging Behaviour Trees, as we can simply only show the relevant information at the time that it was being executed.

36
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:19.370
Here we've got an example of a simple Behaviour Tree, in which the AI agent can either move towards the player and attack them,

37
00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.370
or if they've not yet perceived a player, they will patrol randomly until they do perceive one.

38
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:27.430
Behaviour Tree branches are executed in order.

39
00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:35.159
When authoring your own Behaviour Trees, you'll notice that all of the nodes have been assigned a number when they're placed in the graph.

40
00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:39.159
This numerical value tells us the order in which these nodes will be executed.

41
00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:44.159
So at the higher level, the Behaviour Tree executes top to bottom, whilst on a branch level we execute left to right.

42
00:03:44.159 --> 00:03:48.189
This is critical to understand when authoring your own Behaviour Trees.

43
00:03:48.189 --> 00:03:53.189
You wouldn't want an AI agent to prefer patrolling to chasing and engaging with the player.

44
00:03:53.189 --> 00:03:58.669
It's also important to understand that nodes in the Behaviour Tree can be moved around and will update their execution order

45
00:03:58.669 --> 00:04:04.669
based on their position at runtime, so clean and well spaced out Behaviour Trees are a must.

46
00:04:04.669 --> 00:04:10.669
So on the previous slide, Behaviour Trees come with their own editor graph, and as such have their own graph nodes.

47
00:04:10.669 --> 00:04:19.730
Behaviour Trees are made up of five types of nodes, that being the root node, composite nodes, task nodes, decorator nodes and service nodes.

48
00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:21.730
So let's take a look at what each one of these do.

49
00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:25.959
The root node is the default node found in all Behaviour Trees,

50
00:04:25.959 --> 00:04:29.959
and it serves as the starting point for a Behaviour Tree, and thus cannot be deleted.

51
00:04:29.959 --> 00:04:34.079
This unique node within the tree has got a few special rules.

52
00:04:34.079 --> 00:04:37.079
Firstly, the root node can only have one connection,

53
00:04:37.079 --> 00:04:42.079
and if that connection to the root node is removed, then the entire tree is disabled.

54
00:04:42.079 --> 00:04:46.560
Next, it does not support attaching of decorators or service nodes.

55
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.560
And lastly, the root node does not have any inherent properties.

56
00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.560
However, when you select it, you'll be shown the properties of the Behaviour Tree on the whole.

57
00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:00.660
As we can see with this example, selecting the root node allows us to set the Behaviour Tree's blackboard.

58
00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:05.170
So now we've got our root node, but in its own right it doesn't really do a lot.

59
00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.170
This is where the composite nodes come in.

60
00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:10.170
Composite nodes define the root of a branch.

61
00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:14.620
As well as this, they also determine the rules for how a branch is executed,

62
00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:19.620
and we'll see how each one of the composite nodes goes on to define these in the following slides.

63
00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.839
Another important thing to note about composite nodes

64
00:05:21.839 --> 00:05:25.839
is that they are the only type of node that can be attached to that root node.

65
00:05:25.839 --> 00:05:30.199
Composite nodes support both decorator and service nodes,

66
00:05:30.199 --> 00:05:33.199
and decorators allow us to control how we either enter the branch,

67
00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:35.199
or even cancel out mid execution.

68
00:05:35.199 --> 00:05:40.449
Services will only ever be active if the children of the composite node are being executed.

69
00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:44.449
This is important to understand when we come to actually placing our service nodes.

70
00:05:44.449 --> 00:05:47.959
There are three types of composite nodes that we can use,

71
00:05:47.959 --> 00:05:51.959
that being the selector, the sequence, and the simple parallel.

72
00:05:51.959 --> 00:05:54.509
Let's find out how each of these differ,

73
00:05:54.509 --> 00:05:57.509
and what they offer to the engineer when developing our AI.

74
00:05:57.509 --> 00:06:00.279
Let's start with the selector node.

75
00:06:00.279 --> 00:06:03.980
The selector node will execute its children from left to right,

76
00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:07.980
and it will stop executing when one of its children succeeds in its task.

77
00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:12.139
If a child succeeds, then the selector node itself succeeds.

78
00:06:12.139 --> 00:06:16.139
If all of its children fail, then the selector node itself will fail.

79
00:06:16.139 --> 00:06:18.680
We can see as an example on the right

80
00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:22.680
that the selector composite node will attempt to execute the first wait task,

81
00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:27.680
however its decorator will cause it to fail after one second, thus failing the task.

82
00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:28.680
Instead of failing straight away,

83
00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:32.680
the selector node will execute the secondary wait task and thus succeed.

84
00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:36.290
If we were to place a secondary task ahead of the first,

85
00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:40.290
we would see that the task would succeed and the second would not run.

86
00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:43.509
On the left, we can see that this composite node

87
00:06:43.509 --> 00:06:46.509
also exposes two variables to developers,

88
00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:50.149
the first being applied decorator scope.

89
00:06:50.149 --> 00:06:52.149
If set, all decorators in the branch below

90
00:06:52.149 --> 00:06:55.149
will be removed when execution flow leaves this branch,

91
00:06:55.149 --> 00:06:59.600
but decorators on this node in particular will not be affected.

92
00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:00.600
Lastly, we have the node name,

93
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.600
and this is just the readable name that this node should be assigned

94
00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.000
when being displayed in the behavior graph editor.

95
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.000
It's usually a good idea to name your composite nodes

96
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.000
as it acts as a way to identify what this branch of your behavior tree is trying to do,

97
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.000
especially useful when your behavior trees start to grow in size.

98
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.089
Selectors are ideal for branches in which any sub-branch may perform a task,

99
00:07:23.089 --> 00:07:26.089
but it does not matter which one of them is actually executed.

100
00:07:26.089 --> 00:07:30.600
Next, we have sequence nodes.

101
00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:34.600
Sequences also execute their children from left to right, much like the selector,

102
00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:38.600
but a sequence node will stop executing once one of its children fails.

103
00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.600
If a child fails, the whole sequence node will fail.

104
00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.110
Conversely, if all the sequences' children succeed,

105
00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.110
then the sequence will succeed.

106
00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:50.490
In the example on the right, we can see there are three wait tasks,

107
00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:53.490
with each executing one after the other.

108
00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:56.589
If we were to place a similar condition to any of the other task nodes

109
00:07:56.589 --> 00:07:58.589
like we did in the selector example,

110
00:07:58.589 --> 00:08:00.589
we would see that the sequence would fail immediately

111
00:08:00.589 --> 00:08:03.589
and not execute any of the children following the task that failed.

112
00:08:03.589 --> 00:08:07.000
Sequences are ideal for things such as moving towards a target,

113
00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:11.000
as we would likely run an environment query to find out a location to move to,

114
00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.000
then proceed to that location.

115
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.480
On the left, we can see a similar set of properties

116
00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.480
that we would see in the selector node,

117
00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:19.480
and they do exactly the same thing.

118
00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:23.189
Lastly, we have the simple parallel node.

119
00:08:23.189 --> 00:08:26.189
The simple parallel node allows a single main task node

120
00:08:26.189 --> 00:08:29.189
to be executed alongside a full tree.

121
00:08:29.189 --> 00:08:30.189
When the main task finishes,

122
00:08:30.189 --> 00:08:33.190
depending on what finish mode that we've got selected,

123
00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:36.190
the other tree will either be aborted immediately,

124
00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:38.190
or will simply wait until it finishes.

125
00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.639
Simple parallel nodes are great for things such as

126
00:08:41.639 --> 00:08:44.960
moving continuously whilst also shooting.

127
00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.960
Decorator nodes are the next type of node that we need to look at.

128
00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.960
They can be attached to either composite or task nodes,

129
00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:52.960
but they cannot be placed on their own.

130
00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.179
They define whether a node or branch can be executed at all,

131
00:08:57.179 --> 00:09:01.600
and they're also known as conditionals in other behavior tree systems.

132
00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.600
Lastly, decorator nodes can also be used to abort parts of a behavior tree,

133
00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.600
allowing us to bail out of the execution of a branch

134
00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.600
midway through that execution.

135
00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.399
So, on to task nodes.

136
00:09:12.399 --> 00:09:15.809
The task nodes are nodes that do things,

137
00:09:15.809 --> 00:09:19.809
so for example, move on an AI or editing a blackboard key.

138
00:09:19.809 --> 00:09:23.100
Tasks support both decorator and service nodes,

139
00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:25.100
and other than composite nodes,

140
00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.100
task nodes are the only other node type

141
00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:30.100
that can be placed down onto the behavior graph itself.

142
00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:33.710
There's a wide choice of tasks already implemented in Unreal,

143
00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:35.710
one of which we can already see in the example below,

144
00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:37.710
that being the move to task.

145
00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:40.190
Much like decorator nodes,

146
00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:44.190
we can create our own task nodes in both Blueprint and in C++.

147
00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:46.190
And lastly, we're going to come to services.

148
00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:50.279
Services are nodes that will execute at a defined frequency

149
00:09:50.279 --> 00:09:52.279
when their branch is being executed.

150
00:09:52.279 --> 00:09:54.509
Services can be used to perform checks

151
00:09:54.509 --> 00:09:56.509
or update data about the world

152
00:09:56.509 --> 00:09:58.509
that can then be used in place of parallel nodes

153
00:09:58.509 --> 00:10:01.509
that you might have otherwise used in other behavior tree systems.

154
00:10:01.509 --> 00:10:05.639
Unreal comes with two services supported out of the box,

155
00:10:05.639 --> 00:10:07.639
the first being Default Focus.

156
00:10:07.639 --> 00:10:11.799
This service will set the focus actor directly on the AI controller.

157
00:10:11.799 --> 00:10:13.960
This can be especially useful

158
00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:16.960
for getting hold of a set actor in Blueprint or C++,

159
00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:19.960
instead of having to access the data directly on the blackboard.

160
00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:23.470
The second is RunEQS.

161
00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.470
This service can be used to regularly run

162
00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:28.470
an environment query system template as an assigned interval

163
00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:31.879
and will update the given blackboard value with the result.

164
00:10:31.879 --> 00:10:34.879
Lastly, it's important to note that although services do tick,

165
00:10:34.879 --> 00:10:36.879
you don't have to use them in that way.

166
00:10:36.879 --> 00:10:38.950
Services come with functions that allow developers

167
00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:41.950
to know when a service has come into relevancy of the tree

168
00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:43.950
and when it has ceased to be relevant,

169
00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:45.950
so they don't have to be ticking all of the time.

170
00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:49.490
So now that we know all the different types of nodes that we have access to,

171
00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.490
we should discuss how you utilize memory in the behavior tree.

172
00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:55.750
Behavior tree nodes exist as shared objects,

173
00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:58.750
meaning that all AI agents using the same behavior tree

174
00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.750
will share the same single set of node instances.

175
00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:04.750
This approach has a two-fold effect.

176
00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:07.750
The first is that it improves CPU performance,

177
00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:09.750
and secondly, it reduces memory usage.

178
00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:11.750
It does, however, come with a caveat,

179
00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:15.750
that being that the shared nodes cannot store agent-specific data in their headers.

180
00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:20.769
However, if we do find ourselves needing to store agent-specific data,

181
00:11:20.769 --> 00:11:23.769
Unreal offers support to do so in three different ways.

182
00:11:23.769 --> 00:11:26.769
So let's cover the first way in which you can do so.

183
00:11:26.769 --> 00:11:28.769
That would be instancing your nodes.

184
00:11:28.769 --> 00:11:31.090
Behavior tree nodes expose out a bool

185
00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:34.090
that allows us as developers to instance the node,

186
00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:37.090
and this is the bCreateNodeInstance parameter.

187
00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:40.220
If this is set to true, then this will grant each AI agent

188
00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:43.220
using this behavior tree a unique instance of that node.

189
00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:47.049
This will, of course, allow us to store some agent-specific data

190
00:11:47.049 --> 00:11:51.049
with the downside of actually having a higher memory and CPU cost

191
00:11:51.049 --> 00:11:53.049
to a non-instance version of the node.

192
00:11:53.049 --> 00:11:57.080
There are examples of usage of these node types

193
00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:59.080
within Unreal's own implementation,

194
00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:03.080
such as the play animation task and the run behavior dynamic task.

195
00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:05.840
The second way in which we can get around this

196
00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:07.840
is by using the Blackboard storage.

197
00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.200
The Blackboard storage can be accessed within nodes

198
00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:12.200
to get agent-specific data,

199
00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:14.200
and key functions in our nodes send through

200
00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:18.289
the AI agent's behavior tree component as one of its parameters.

201
00:12:18.289 --> 00:12:21.289
As developers, we can simply expose an fName property

202
00:12:21.289 --> 00:12:23.289
to make use of a Blackboard key selector

203
00:12:23.289 --> 00:12:27.419
to allow us to get and set data from and to the Blackboard.

204
00:12:27.419 --> 00:12:29.419
It's important to note that this approach

205
00:12:29.419 --> 00:12:31.419
can only support Blackboard data types,

206
00:12:31.419 --> 00:12:34.059
which we're going to discuss in just a second.

207
00:12:34.059 --> 00:12:37.320
And lastly, we've got the node memory.

208
00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:40.320
Unreal allows developers to create a custom struct or class

209
00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.570
to store variables inside the node's memory.

210
00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:45.570
Many of the functions in behavior tree nodes

211
00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.570
pass through a Uint8 pointer parameter,

212
00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:50.570
and this parameter indicates the block of memory

213
00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:53.570
that has been allocated to our AI agent.

214
00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:55.570
The get instance memory size function,

215
00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:58.570
which must be implemented if we're going to use the memory approach,

216
00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:02.049
will dictate the size of this block of memory.

217
00:13:02.049 --> 00:13:06.049
Nodes will allocate this amount of memory for each AI agent,

218
00:13:06.049 --> 00:13:08.049
and the node memory is stored in a single continuous block

219
00:13:08.049 --> 00:13:11.179
to optimize performance.

220
00:13:11.179 --> 00:13:13.179
However, it's very important to keep in mind

221
00:13:13.179 --> 00:13:16.179
that node memory is not part of the U object ecosystem,

222
00:13:16.179 --> 00:13:19.529
so our memory is not visible to the garbage collector.

223
00:13:19.529 --> 00:13:22.529
It's highly recommended that when dealing with reflection types

224
00:13:22.529 --> 00:13:24.529
that we make use of the Tweek object pointer

225
00:13:24.529 --> 00:13:26.600
to hold your variable.

226
00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:28.600
We can see an example of how this is done

227
00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:29.600
in the next slide,

228
00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:31.600
and this example is lifted from the move to task

229
00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:34.840
already implemented in Unreal.

230
00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:37.840
So now we've covered the simulated will of the AI

231
00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:39.840
and the brains of the AI.

232
00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:43.129
Now we need to discuss the memory of the AI.

233
00:13:43.129 --> 00:13:45.129
The blackboard is a special asset type

234
00:13:45.129 --> 00:13:47.129
that is created specifically to work in conjunction

235
00:13:47.129 --> 00:13:49.159
within the behavior tree.

236
00:13:49.159 --> 00:13:53.159
It's used to store information in the shape of a blackboard key.

237
00:13:53.159 --> 00:13:55.159
This information can then be queried by the behavior tree

238
00:13:55.159 --> 00:13:58.360
at runtime to make informed decisions.

239
00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.360
A blackboard can store up to 255 entries

240
00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.360
and they can also support parent blackboards.

241
00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:07.360
This means we can inherit keys from other blackboards.

242
00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:11.190
The blackboard component offers programmers the entry way

243
00:14:11.190 --> 00:14:15.190
for querying and saving data to and from the blackboard respectively.

244
00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:17.860
The blackboard component is strongly paired

245
00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:19.919
to the behavior tree component.

246
00:14:19.919 --> 00:14:21.919
It will cache the component and programmers can retrieve

247
00:14:21.919 --> 00:14:25.919
the brain component of the AI directly from the blackboard component.

248
00:14:25.919 --> 00:14:29.240
The blackboard component also follows a similar pattern

249
00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:32.690
to the behavior tree component in terms of creation.

250
00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:35.690
It's not present by default on the AI controller

251
00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:37.690
and much like the behavior tree component,

252
00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:40.690
it can either be added via blueprints or C++.

253
00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:42.690
However, this isn't necessary as one will be created

254
00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.690
when the AI logic is triggered to start.

255
00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:46.690
But it's also important to note that the blackboard

256
00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:48.690
is not essential to running the AI logic

257
00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:51.679
as we mentioned earlier.

258
00:14:51.679 --> 00:14:53.679
Again, much like the behavior tree component,

259
00:14:53.679 --> 00:14:56.679
the blackboard component does expose a blackboard asset

260
00:14:56.679 --> 00:14:59.159
to the editor which can be plugged in directly.

261
00:14:59.159 --> 00:15:02.159
The blackboard component also offers a unique callback functionality

262
00:15:02.159 --> 00:15:05.159
that allows objects to register as observers

263
00:15:05.159 --> 00:15:07.159
of a particular blackboard key

264
00:15:07.159 --> 00:15:10.159
so that they can be notified when that blackboard value has changed.

265
00:15:10.159 --> 00:15:13.679
A blackboard can store the following data types.

266
00:15:13.679 --> 00:15:16.679
We can store a lot of our expected basic types

267
00:15:16.679 --> 00:15:20.679
such as vector, rotator, bool, int32, float, and string

268
00:15:20.679 --> 00:15:22.679
as well as enum.

269
00:15:22.679 --> 00:15:24.679
But we can also store some much more unreal specific types

270
00:15:24.679 --> 00:15:28.679
such as the fname, uobject, and uclass.

271
00:15:28.679 --> 00:15:30.679
If you wish to look at how data for each of these keys

272
00:15:30.679 --> 00:15:32.679
is actually manipulated, then we'd highly recommend you

273
00:15:32.679 --> 00:15:35.679
take a look at the BlackboardKeyAllTypes header

274
00:15:35.679 --> 00:15:37.679
which will give us a handy include

275
00:15:37.679 --> 00:15:39.899
for all of the types that we listed above.

276
00:15:39.899 --> 00:15:41.899
Lastly, it's important to note that a blackboard

277
00:15:41.899 --> 00:15:43.899
cannot store arrays of data.

278
00:15:43.899 --> 00:15:46.899
The blackboard works off of a single block of memory

279
00:15:46.899 --> 00:15:48.899
and data types are stored with offsets

280
00:15:48.899 --> 00:15:50.899
based off of their data size.
